@startuml

' =============================================================
' UC-005 Domain Glossary (CIM) - English
'
' MDA Level: Computation Independent Model
' Perspective: Business / Conceptualization
'
' This diagram defines the BUSINESS CONCEPTS at Slottet using
' UML class notation. It is NOT a data model — there are no
' types, no IDs, no methods. Each box is a concept that exists
' in the real world at Slottet, regardless of any IT system.
'
' Notice: There is no "PhoneAssignment" entity here. That is a
' system construct that emerges in the PIM. In the real domain,
' a Caregiver simply CARRIES a Work Phone on a Shift.
' =============================================================

title UC-005 Domain Glossary — Phone List Coordination (CIM)

skinparam classBackgroundColor #FFF9E6
skinparam classBorderColor #B8860B
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam classFontStyle bold
skinparam classAttributeFontSize 12

' --- Core domain concepts ---
class "Caregiver" as Caregiver {
    A staff member who provides
    care to residents during a shift
}

class "Shift" as Shift {
    A working period at Slottet
    Types: Day, Evening, Night
}

class "Work Phone" as Phone {
    A physical phone used by caregivers
    for communication on the premises
    Numbers are fixed and assigned
    to the facility, not to people
}

class "Resident" as Resident {
    A person receiving care
    at Slottet
}

class "Slottet" as Slottet {
    24-hour residential care facility
    for residents with special needs
}

' --- Domain relationships (business language, not system-speak) ---
Caregiver "1..*" -- "1" Shift : works on >
Caregiver "1" -- "0..1" Phone : carries during shift >
Caregiver "1..*" -- "1..*" Resident : cares for >
Slottet "1" *-- "1..*" Caregiver : employs
Slottet "1" *-- "7" Phone : owns
Slottet "1" *-- "1..*" Resident : houses

note bottom of Phone
    <b>Fixed phone numbers at Slottet:</b>
    41522, 41523, 41524, 41525, 41526, 41527, 41529

    These numbers belong to <i>Slottet</i>, not to people.
    A Caregiver carries a phone <i>during</i> a shift,
    but the phone itself stays at Slottet.
end note

note bottom of Shift
    <b>Shift types:</b>
    - Day
    - Evening
    - Night

    A shift defines <i>when</i> work happens.
    Each Caregiver works one Shift at a time.
end note

note as N1
    <b>What this glossary does NOT contain:</b>

    No "PhoneAssignment" — that is a system construct
    that appears in PIM. In the real world there is only
    a relationship: a Caregiver carries a Work Phone
    during a Shift. No third "thing" exists.

    No data types (Guid, String, int) — those are
    platform-specific and appear in PSM.

    No methods or operations — concepts don't <i>do</i>
    things, they <i>are</i> things.

    No primary keys or foreign keys — these are
    database concerns that appear in PSM.
end note

@enduml
